<SiteLayout
  title="The Ghost in the DNS Zone"
  description="Notes from Daph’s October 2026 Cloudflare and DNS investigation: a legacy GoDaddy-pay CNAME clue, reconnaissance, and following the evidence without overclaiming."
>
  <article aria-labelledby="article-title">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">null route queen / security / October 2026</p>
      <h1 id="article-title" class="page-title">The Ghost in the DNS Zone</h1>
      <p class="intro">
        An unfamiliar DNS record can make a quiet zone feel haunted. During my
        October 2026 Cloudflare and DNS reconnaissance investigation, a legacy
        GoDaddy-pay CNAME became a clue worth following. The useful question was:
        what does the evidence actually support?
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="starting-point">
      <h2 id="starting-point">Start with the clue</h2>
      <p>
        The starting point was Cloudflare and DNS evidence, with a legacy
        GoDaddy-pay CNAME in the picture. That association gave the investigation
        a direction: understand the record’s purpose and its relationship to
        the site’s history before treating it as a security finding.
      </p>
      <p>
        A CNAME maps one DNS name to another. Its presence alone does not explain
        who created it, whether a service is still needed, or whether anyone
        has abused it. “GoDaddy-pay” is the clue described here; this account
        does not reproduce an exact hostname or target.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="separate-evidence">
      <h2 id="separate-evidence">Keep the evidence and the explanation separate</h2>
      <p>
        A legacy service association is a plausible explanation for an unfamiliar
        record. Plausible is a place to begin checking. It is not a substitute
        for confirming the record’s history, current destination, and intended use.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reconnaissance needs the same discipline. A request can show that someone
        tried a path or hostname. It does not, by itself, establish access,
        compromise, or a connection to a particular DNS record. Putting two
        observations next to each other does not establish cause and effect.
      </p>
      <p>
        This account does not establish an attacker’s identity, a successful
        intrusion, or a subdomain takeover. The legacy CNAME is a lead, not
        evidence of any of those outcomes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="workable-path">
      <h2 id="workable-path">Understand the risk before changing the zone</h2>
      <p>
        The practical lesson is to preserve the evidence and identify dependencies
        before making a change. Weird does not automatically mean malicious.
        Weird means: go look. An unfamiliar record might be obsolete; it might
        also support something that still matters.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before deleting the weird-looking thing and congratulating yourself,
        make sure Future You is not about to inherit an outage.
      </p>

      <p>The checklist I would carry into the next investigation:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Record the DNS name, type, target, and observation time before editing.</li>
        <li>Check authoritative DNS and distinguish current answers from historical evidence.</li>
        <li>Confirm the associated service and whether the record is still required.</li>
        <li>Read request outcomes alongside the requests; keep attempted access separate from successful access.</li>
        <li>Choose a justified change, preserve a rollback path, and verify the affected service afterward.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        These are investigation steps, not a claim that every check or a DNS
        cleanup was completed in this case. The evidence summarized here does
        not establish a remediation outcome.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="card starter" aria-labelledby="lesson">
      <h2 id="lesson">Let the evidence set the size of the story</h2>
      <p>
        The ghost in this story is the trace of an older service in a DNS zone.
        Following that trace is useful even when it does not end in a dramatic
        incident report. Knowing what remains uncertain is part of understanding
        the system.
      </p>
      <p>
        Investigate first. Understand the risk. Explain what the evidence supports.
        Then choose the safest workable path. The DNS zone does not need a
        scarier story than the facts can carry.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>

